const UNIT = {
  "gradeLevel": "10",
  "subject": "Essential Mathematics",
  "strand": "Strand 2.0: Measurements and Geometry",
  "substrand": "Sub-Strand 2.3: Trigonometry",
  "totalDuration": "8 lessons × 40 minutes = 320 minutes total",
  "content": "• Identification of sides of a right-angled triangle (hypotenuse, opposite, adjacent) relative to a specific acute angle\n• Sine, cosine and tangent ratios of acute angles in a right-angled triangle\n• Sines and cosines of complementary angles and their relationship\n• Application of trigonometric ratios to angles of elevation\n• Application of trigonometric ratios to angles of depression\n• Construction and use of a simple clinometer to measure angles of elevation and depression\n• Use of trigonometric tables and digital tools to find trigonometric ratios\n• Real-life applications of trigonometry in the immediate environment",
  "learningOutcomes": "By the end of the sub-strand, the learner should be able to:\na) identify the sides of a right-angled triangle in relation to a particular angle,\nb) determine the sine, cosine and tangent of acute angles in a right-angled triangle,\nc) relate sines and cosines of complementary angles,\nd) apply trigonometric ratios to angles of elevation and depression,\ne) recognise the use of trigonometry in real-life situations.",
  "coreCompetencies": "• Self-efficacy: The learner makes observations and establishes the relationships between the sine and cosine ratios of complementary angles, building confidence in their own mathematical reasoning as they generate and verify the pattern independently.\n• Learning to learn: The learner learns collaboratively while constructing a simple clinometer using locally available materials (cardboard, straw, string, protractor) and using it as a hands-on learning resource for measuring angles of elevation and depression in the school environment.",
  "values": "• Love: The learner demonstrates a caring attitude towards others when using a simple clinometer to measure the angle of elevation and depression of different objects in the immediate environment, ensuring every group member has a turn and that measurements are taken safely and considerately.\n• Responsibility: The learner takes care of instruments such as rulers, geometrical sets, graph paper and digital devices used during trigonometry activities, returning them in good condition and ensuring shared resources are available for peers.",
  "sep": "• Asking Questions and Defining Problems: Learners articulate what information is needed (angle, known side) to find an unknown height or distance, framing trigonometry as a tool for answering real measurement questions about their environment.\n• Planning and Carrying Out Investigations: Learners design and carry out a clinometer investigation — identifying objects to measure, deciding on measurement positions, recording angle and distance data, and checking results for consistency.\n• Using Mathematics and Computational Thinking: Learners select and apply the appropriate trigonometric ratio (sin, cos, tan) to compute unknown sides and angles, evaluate answers for reasonableness, and use digital tools (calculators, online resources) to verify computations.\n• Constructing Explanations: Learners use their generated table of complementary angles and their sine/cosine values to construct a reasoned explanation for why sin θ = cos(90° − θ), connecting numerical patterns to geometric reasoning.\n• Obtaining, Evaluating and Communicating Information: Learners present clinometer findings and trigonometric solutions to peers, critique each other's methods, and evaluate the accuracy of real-world measurements against calculated values.",
  "pcis": "• Self-esteem: The learners acquire and strengthen self-esteem while designing and making a simple clinometer using locally available materials (cardboard, string, protractor, drinking straw) and successfully using it as a functional learning resource for measuring real angles in their school environment.\n• Life Skills (Critical Thinking and Problem Solving): Learners apply trigonometric ratios to authentic measurement problems — such as estimating the height of a flagpole, water tank, or school building in Nairobi — developing analytical and decision-making skills transferable to daily life.\n• Education for Sustainable Development: Learners use locally sourced, low-cost materials to construct the clinometer, reinforcing resourcefulness and environmental awareness while achieving a meaningful mathematical learning outcome.",
  "careers": "• Surveying and Land Management: Trigonometric ratios are foundational tools for surveyors who map land parcels across Kenya's counties — from the Rift Valley to the Coast — measuring distances and elevations using clinometers and theodolites.\n• Architecture and Building Construction: Architects and construction engineers in Kenya use angles of elevation, roof pitch calculations and trigonometric ratios when designing and constructing buildings, ensuring structural stability.\n• Aviation and Navigation: Pilots and navigators at airports such as JKIA (Nairobi) and Moi International Airport (Mombasa) apply trigonometry to calculate flight approach angles, altitudes and distances.\n• Sports Coaching and Biomechanics: Sports scientists and coaches working with Kenyan athletes (e.g., track and field at Kipchoge Keino Stadium, Eldoret) use angle analysis to optimise throwing, jumping and running techniques.\n• Photography and Media Technology: Photographers and videographers computing camera angles, framing shots and calculating perspective use principles directly related to trigonometric ratios.\n• Forestry and Environmental Science: Forest officers in Kenya's Aberdare and Mau Forest complexes use clinometers to measure tree heights and terrain slopes — a direct application of angles of elevation.",
  "focus": "This unit introduces learners to trigonometry through a practical, investigation-led approach anchored in the Kenyan built and natural environment. Learners move from identifying the sides of a right-angled triangle, through deriving and applying the three trigonometric ratios, to discovering the complementary-angle relationship and finally deploying their knowledge to solve real problems involving angles of elevation and depression using a student-built clinometer. Throughout the 8 lessons, every concept is connected back to the anchoring phenomenon of estimating the height of an inaccessible structure using only a measured distance and an angle, reinforcing trigonometry as a practical, empowering tool rather than an abstract set of rules.",
  "phenomenon": "ANCHORING PHENOMENON — \"How tall is the water tank without climbing it?\"\n\nAt many Kenyan schools and homesteads, a large elevated water tank (sometimes mounted on a steel frame 4–8 metres high) is a familiar sight. Students can clearly see the tank from the ground but cannot climb it or use a tape measure along its height directly.\n\nThe teacher presents this puzzling scenario: Two students stand at different positions on the ground, both a known horizontal distance from the base of the water-tank tower. Each student uses a simple angle-measuring device (a protractor and string) to sight the top of the tank and records a different angle. Without any additional climbing or measuring, can we use ONLY these two pieces of information — the horizontal distance and the measured angle — to calculate exactly how high the top of the tank is above the ground?\n\nThis is puzzling because:\n(1) The students never measured the height directly, yet they can calculate it precisely.\n(2) Two students at different distances get different angles but should arrive at the SAME height — a powerful consistency check.\n(3) The same method, scaled up, is how surveyors map the Kenyan highlands, how engineers design the Thika Superhighway overpasses, and how foresters in the Mau Forest measure tree heights without felling them.\n\nThe phenomenon is revisited and refined in every lesson: learners begin unable to explain it, gradually acquire the ratio tools (sin, cos, tan), discover the complementary-angle shortcut, build their own clinometer, and in the final lesson use the clinometer outdoors to measure a real structure and confirm their prediction — closing the explanatory loop completely.",
  "supportingPhenomena": "• THE LEANING SIGN BOARD: A rectangular signboard on a tilted pole outside the school gate — learners notice the pole makes an acute angle with the ground. How do we find the actual height the sign reaches without measuring along the pole? (Supports: identifying opposite/adjacent/hypotenuse and setting up a tan ratio.)\n• THE SHADOW PUZZLE: At midday, a vertical maize stalk in the school garden casts a short shadow; in the late afternoon the same stalk casts a much longer shadow. Why does the shadow length change, and can we use the shadow to find the height of the stalk? (Supports: angle of elevation of the sun, tan ratio, and the idea that the same height gives different ratios at different angles.)\n• THE MIRROR ON THE FLOOR: A small mirror is placed flat on the ground between a learner and the base of a wall. The learner steps back until they can just see the top of the wall in the mirror. The triangle formed is similar to the wall triangle. How is this related to the ratios we computed? (Supports: connecting similar triangles to constant trigonometric ratios.)\n• THE STAIRCASE RAMP: A newly built concrete ramp at a Nairobi hospital rises 1.2 m over a horizontal run of 4.8 m. Is this ramp accessible for wheelchair users (angle < 14°)? (Supports: applying arctan/inverse trigonometry and angles of elevation in a real accessibility-design context.)",
  "drivingQuestion": "DRIVING QUESTION: How can we use the angles and one side of a right-angled triangle to find any unknown height or distance in our environment — without ever measuring it directly?\n\nKICD KEY INQUIRY QUESTION:\n1. Why is trigonometry important?",
  "storylineThread": "Lesson 1 — PREDICT: \"The Water Tank Problem\" (Anchoring Phenomenon Launch)\nLearners observe a photograph/diagram of the school water tank or a similar elevated structure. In small groups they predict: \"What information would you need, and how would you use it, to find the height of the tank without climbing?\" Groups record predictions on sticky notes for the Driving Question Board (DQB). Teacher introduces vocabulary: right-angled triangle, hypotenuse, opposite side, adjacent side. Learners physically identify these sides in right-angled shapes around the classroom (door frames, window corners, book corners) with reference to a named acute angle. DQB is set up with the driving question posted prominently.\n\nLesson 2 — OBSERVE (Part 1): Deriving the Three Trigonometric Ratios\nLearners use rulers and protractors to draw three different right-angled triangles each with the same acute angle (e.g., 35°) but different sizes. They measure the opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse for each triangle and compute the three ratios (opp/hyp, adj/hyp, opp/adj) in a table. Learners notice each ratio remains constant regardless of triangle size — a key discovery. Teacher names the ratios: sine, cosine, tangent. Learners use a scientific calculator to verify their measured ratios against calculator values. Connection to phenomenon: \"These constant ratios are exactly what let us calculate the tank height from any distance!\"\n\nLesson 3 — OBSERVE (Part 2): Sine and Cosine of Complementary Angles\nLearners generate a table of angle pairs that sum to 90° (e.g., 20° & 70°, 35° & 55°, 45° & 45°) and record their sines and cosines. They discover that sin θ = cos(90° − θ) and cos θ = sin(90° − θ). Learners construct an explanation for this pattern using a right-angled triangle diagram, noting that the \"opposite\" for one angle is the \"adjacent\" for its complement. DQB is updated: new understanding posted, new questions raised (e.g., \"Does this work for ALL angles?\").\n\nLesson 4 — EXPLAIN (Part 1): Applying Trigonometric Ratios to Find Unknown Sides\nGuided practice: learners use sin, cos and tan to calculate unknown sides in right-angled triangles drawn on graph paper. Problems are set in Kenyan contexts — length of a roof rafter in Kisumu, distance across a stream in Kericho, height of a mango tree in a Mombasa compound. Learners practise selecting the correct ratio, setting up the equation and solving. Peer checking of answers. Connection to phenomenon: learners set up and solve the water-tank calculation for the first time using a given angle and distance.\n\nLesson 5 — OBSERVE (Part 3): Angle of Elevation — Clinometer Construction (Project Lesson)\nLearners construct a simple clinometer using a cardboard protractor, drinking straw, string and a small weight (stone or metal washer). Teacher models how to sight along the straw to an elevated object and read the angle of elevation. Learners practise sighting objects of known approximate height in the classroom. Discussion: what is the \"angle of elevation\"? How does it relate to the right-angled triangle? DQB updated with new vocabulary and connections.\n\nLesson 6 — OBSERVE (Part 4): Angle of Depression and Outdoor Measurement\nLearners take their clinometers outdoors. Working in groups of 3–4, they measure the angle of elevation to the top of the school flagpole, water tank, or a tall tree, and record the horizontal distance from the base. They also identify and measure an angle of depression (e.g., sighting from a raised veranda down to a ground-level marker). Data is recorded on structured worksheets. Back in the classroom, each group calculates the height of the object using the appropriate trigonometric ratio. Groups compare results and discuss sources of error.\n\nLesson 7 — EXPLAIN (Part 2): Problem Solving — Angles of Elevation and Depression\nStructured problem-solving lesson using multi-step real-life scenarios: a Rift Valley farmer estimating the height of a cliff face, a diver at Watamu estimating depth from a boat, a construction worker in Nairobi checking the angle of a ramp. Learners work individually then compare with a partner. Common errors (confusing opposite/adjacent, not identifying the correct reference angle) are addressed explicitly. DQB is updated: learners move sticky notes from \"questions\" to \"answered\" and add new refined understandings. Learners revisit their original predictions from Lesson 1 and evaluate them.\n\nLesson 8 — MODEL BUILDING & SUMMATIVE CLOSURE: \"Solving the Water Tank Problem\"\nLearners use all accumulated knowledge to produce a final written and diagrammatic solution to the original anchoring phenomenon. Each learner draws a labelled diagram of the water-tank scenario, identifies the right-angled triangle, selects and applies the correct trigonometric ratio, calculates the height, and writes a clear explanation of their method. Groups present their clinometer project findings (angle, distance, calculated height, reflection on accuracy). Teacher facilitates a whole-class DQB review — all original questions are revisited and either answered or identified as leading to future learning (e.g., trigonometry of non-right-angled triangles). Formative assessment via class activity and portfolio entry."
};

