        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'For the last time — look at our Driving Question Board. In groups of four, review your assigned column. For each question, decide: green — fully answered, yellow — partially answered, orange — still open. You have 5 minutes.' Assign columns by pointing to groups. Distribute stickers. After 5 minutes: 'Each group, one sentence — what was the status of your questions?' Cold-call each group. After all groups have reported, say: 'Now, together, we are going to write the official answer to our driving question: How can we use the angles and one side of a right-angled triangle to find any unknown height or distance in our environment — without ever measuring it directly? Someone start me a sentence.' Wait 10 seconds, then cold-call one learner. Build the statement collaboratively, pressing for precision: 'Can we make that more specific? What ratio did we use? What did the horizontal distance represent?' Write the final 2–3 sentence statement in large text on the board. Say: 'Copy this into your book. It is your Final Answer.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Collaborative DQB Closure with Sticker Coding: the sticker-coding activity makes the growth of learner understanding visible and concrete — green stickers show questions that were mysterious in Lesson 1 but are now fully resolved. The collaborative authorship of the DQB Summary Statement requires learners to synthesise, negotiate precise language and commit to a shared explanation, which is the highest level of the sensemaking progression across the unit.",
