      "prompt": "In your own words, explain why it is difficult or impossible to measure the height of an elevated water tank directly using a tape measure. What information CAN you collect from the ground without climbing? Describe the two pieces of information that make the trigonometric method work.",
      "exemplar": "Measuring the height of a water tank directly is impractical because the tank is elevated on a steel frame several metres above the ground, and climbing it — or stretching a tape measure vertically along its full height — is unsafe and often not allowed. However, from a safe position on the ground, you CAN measure two things accurately: (1) the horizontal distance from where you are standing to the base of the tank's support frame, and (2) the angle of elevation — the angle between your line of sight to the top of the tank and the horizontal ground. These two pieces of information, a known side and a known angle in a right-angled triangle, are all that trigonometry needs to calculate the unknown height precisely — without ever touching the tank."
