      "prompt": "Draw and label a right-angled triangle that represents the water-tank situation. Identify the angle of elevation (θ), the horizontal distance (adjacent side), and the height of the tank (opposite side). Then explain which trigonometric ratio — sine, cosine, or tangent — is the most useful here and why. Write the formula you would use to calculate the height, and show a worked example using an angle of 35° and a horizontal distance of 12 metres.",
      "exemplar": "In the right-angled triangle formed by the water-tank situation: the horizontal ground forms the ADJACENT side, the vertical height of the tank forms the OPPOSITE side, and the imaginary line from your eye to the top of the tank is the HYPOTENUSE. The angle of elevation θ sits between the adjacent side and the hypotenuse at ground level.\n\nThe most useful ratio here is the TANGENT, because we know the adjacent side (horizontal distance) and we want to find the opposite side (height) — and tangent connects exactly those two sides:\n\n  tan(θ) = opposite / adjacent  →  height = adjacent × tan(θ)\n\nWorked example:\n  θ = 35°,  horizontal distance = 12 m\n  height = 12 × tan(35°)\n  height = 12 × 0.7002\n  height ≈ 8.40 metres\n\nSo the top of the tank is approximately 8.40 metres above the ground. Note: if your eye level is above the ground (e.g., 1.5 m), you must add that value to your final answer to get the true height from the ground."
