      "exemplar": "Because the water tank has one fixed height, any right-angled triangle drawn from a different position on the ground will have a different base length and a different angle — but the vertical side (the height) stays constant. Trigonometry captures this relationship precisely: as you move farther away, the angle gets smaller, and the larger base length compensates exactly to produce the same height.\n\nSecond worked example:\n  θ = 52°,  horizontal distance = 7.5 m\n  height = 7.5 × tan(52°)\n  height = 7.5 × 1.2799\n  height ≈ 9.60 metres\n\nIf both students' results are close (differences only due to small measurement errors in reading the angle), this confirms that the method is internally consistent and reliable. This consistency is powerful: it means trigonometry is not a lucky guess — it is a dependable mathematical relationship. Kenyan surveyors use exactly this consistency check when mapping land in the Rift Valley; multiple readings from different stations must agree before a boundary is accepted as legal."
