      "exemplar": "Two angles are COMPLEMENTARY when they add up to 90°. In any right-angled triangle, the two non-right angles are always complementary — if one angle is θ, the other is (90° – θ).\n\nThe shortcut is: sin(θ) = cos(90° – θ), and cos(θ) = sin(90° – θ). This means the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement, and vice versa.\n\nWater-tank example:\n  In the triangle with θ = 35° (angle of elevation), the angle at the top of the tank is 90° – 35° = 55°.\n  sin(35°) = cos(55°) ≈ 0.5736\n  cos(35°) = sin(55°) ≈ 0.8192\n\nThis is useful because if a problem gives you the angle at the top of the triangle instead of the base, you do not need to re-draw the triangle — you can simply swap sine and cosine using the complementary relationship. It also helps you check your calculator work: if sin(40°) ≠ cos(50°) on your calculator, you have made an error."
