      "prompt": "Now bring everything together. Answer the driving question fully: 'How can we use the angles and one side of a right-angled triangle to find any unknown height or distance in our environment — without ever measuring it directly?' Reference the water tank, your clinometer investigation, and at least ONE real-world Kenyan application beyond the school compound. Explain why this skill matters.",
      "exemplar": "We can find any unknown height or distance — without measuring it directly — by forming an imaginary right-angled triangle between ourselves, the object, and the ground, then applying a trigonometric ratio to the known side and known angle.\n\nFor the water tank: stand at a measured horizontal distance from the base, use a clinometer (like the one we built from a protractor and string) to measure the angle of elevation to the top of the tank, then calculate: height = horizontal distance × tan(angle of elevation). The height is revealed precisely, with no climbing required.\n\nOur clinometer investigation confirmed this works in practice. When two of us measured from different distances, our calculated heights differed by less than 0.3 metres — a small error caused only by the precision of our protractor reading, not a flaw in the method.\n\nBeyond the school compound, this same approach is used every day across Kenya:\n• Foresters in the Mau Forest measure the height of indigenous trees to estimate timber volume — without felling a single tree.\n• Engineers designing bridges along the Thika Superhighway calculate vertical clearance heights using angle and distance measurements taken from the road.\n• Tea farmers in Kericho use slope angles to plan drainage channels, preventing soil erosion on hillside farms.\n\nTrigonometry matters because it gives us a universal mathematical tool for bridging what we CAN measure (a safe, horizontal distance and a simple angle) to what we CANNOT measure directly (a dangerous or inaccessible height). It turns an impossible physical task into a straightforward calculation — and that is exactly why it is one of the most practical branches of mathematics we will ever study."
